WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.850
This is Chapter 4: PIM for
Microsoft Entra roles as code.

4
00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:14.134
A role setting someone changed in the
portal is a control nobody reviewed,

5
00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:17.646
so this chapter holds each role's
settings in a reviewed file,

6
00:00:17.646 --> 00:00:22.000
compares the file with the tenant, and
changes the tenant only from the file.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.754
We'll start from Microsoft's two samples,
see what they leave out, then walk

8
00:00:26.754 --> 00:00:31.588
the role files, the checks, the diff and
the rollout, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:31.588 --> 00:00:37.090
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.565
Why does this chapter exist?

11
00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:45.052
Because a setting someone changed by hand

12
00:00:45.052 --> 00:00:47.540
in the portal is a
control nobody reviewed.

13
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.877
And because the order of changes matters.

14
00:00:49.877 --> 00:00:54.200
Learn documents a lockout: every
top administrator eligible,

15
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.353
none active, approval required,
and nobody named to approve.

16
00:00:58.353 --> 00:01:02.864
So the chapter won't touch any role
setting until the emergency accounts from

17
00:01:02.864 --> 00:01:04.486
Chapter 2 hold in the tenant,

18
00:01:04.486 --> 00:01:08.364
and it refuses any file that asks
for approval without an approver.

19
00:01:08.364 --> 00:01:10.535
The plan's order is fixed too:

20
00:01:10.535 --> 00:01:14.760
one pilot class first, then
every tier, one at a time.

21
00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.873
Next, which cloud.

22
00:01:19.873 --> 00:01:23.200
Everything here is Microsoft
Graph, version one,

23
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.523
with no Resource Manager calls at all;
Azure resource roles wait for Chapter 6.

24
00:01:28.523 --> 00:01:32.668
This estate's tenant is GCC,
so Graph is the worldwide

25
00:01:32.668 --> 00:01:36.235
host and sign-in is the
worldwide sign-in page.

26
00:01:36.235 --> 00:01:40.551
GCC High and the Defense Department
cloud would use their own hosts,

27
00:01:40.551 --> 00:01:45.098
and every call's Learn page lists the
US Government clouds as available.

28
00:01:45.098 --> 00:01:49.571
One small design choice matters: the
code takes an explicit cloud name

29
00:01:49.571 --> 00:01:54.470
and has no default, so nobody runs it
against the wrong cloud by accident.

30
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.133
Here's the shape of what we're managing.

31
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:03.090
Every Microsoft Entra role
has its own settings,

32
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.970
independent of every other role, and every
assignment of that role follows them.

33
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.989
In Graph, each role gets one policy,
and each policy holds seventeen rules

34
00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:18.007
with fixed names, grouped into
activation, assignment and notification.

35
00:02:18.007 --> 00:02:22.807
This chapter manages the seven activation
and assignment rules in the workbook's

36
00:02:22.807 --> 00:02:25.560
privileged-access design,
the ones on the slide,

37
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.807
and deliberately leaves the
notification rules alone.

38
00:02:28.807 --> 00:02:32.860
Each of those seven becomes a
few lines in a reviewed file.

39
00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.722
The authentication context deserves its
own slide, because it's easy to get wrong.

40
00:02:41.722 --> 00:02:45.762
Learn says the Conditional Access
policy behind the context must

41
00:02:45.762 --> 00:02:49.435
exist and be switched on before
you select the context in PIM.

42
00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:53.405
If no policy targets it,
activation falls back to MFA,

43
00:02:53.405 --> 00:02:58.298
but if the policy is off or report-only,
nothing is enforced at all.

44
00:02:58.298 --> 00:03:01.941
Even with it on, you only get
a prompt on every activation if

45
00:03:01.941 --> 00:03:05.736
that policy asks for it, and a
ten-minute window still applies.

46
00:03:05.736 --> 00:03:09.543
Learn's pages also disagree on
whether the option is in preview,

47
00:03:09.543 --> 00:03:12.310
so the chapter keeps calling it preview.

48
00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.022
Microsoft gives you two samples.

49
00:03:18.022 --> 00:03:22.001
One shows how to find a role's
policy and then update its rules,

50
00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:24.786
six requests, each
carrying the right type.

51
00:03:24.786 --> 00:03:29.510
The other is a tutorial that makes a
group eligible for a role and has a member

52
00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:32.735
activate it, which is exactly
the workbook's pattern:

53
00:03:32.735 --> 00:03:35.585
roles are eligible to a
group, never to a person.

54
00:03:35.585 --> 00:03:38.409
Those samples live only inside web pages,

55
00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:43.885
so the repository pins the pages' source,
unmodified, with hashes a test checks.

56
00:03:43.885 --> 00:03:47.028
Then a harness sends every
request to a fake Graph,

57
00:03:47.028 --> 00:03:49.450
so you can see what they really do.

58
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.788
This is where the samples fall short,
and every row is pinned by a test.

59
00:03:57.788 --> 00:04:01.625
Learn's read and its rule shapes
are good, so the code keeps them.

60
00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:06.729
But there's no sample for the authentication
context rule at all, and the activation

61
00:04:06.729 --> 00:04:11.537
sample relies on MFA, which may not prompt
again in a session that already did it.

62
00:04:11.537 --> 00:04:16.762
Every update names a policy from somebody
else's tenant, so a copied script fails.

63
00:04:16.762 --> 00:04:19.638
And even with the right
policy, the samples leave a

64
00:04:19.638 --> 00:04:22.863
role different from the
program's settings in six rules.

65
00:04:22.863 --> 00:04:25.180
So the code adds a real diff.

66
00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.042
The rest of the gaps are about safety.

67
00:04:31.042 --> 00:04:34.774
Nothing in the samples reads
before writing, offers a dry run,

68
00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:38.431
or reads back afterward, and
nothing checks for the lockout.

69
00:04:38.431 --> 00:04:42.753
The tutorial asks for a year of
eligibility, twice what the program allows

70
00:04:42.753 --> 00:04:46.793
for that tier, and for permissions
far broader than the request needs.

71
00:04:46.793 --> 00:04:49.243
Its clean-up would
delete the wrong object.

72
00:04:49.243 --> 00:04:51.722
So the verdict is clear:
keep Learn's read,

73
00:04:51.722 --> 00:04:56.605
its rule shapes and its write permission,
but don't run the samples as published.

74
00:04:56.605 --> 00:04:59.160
Let's look at what the
code builds instead.

75
00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.235
So what does the code add?

76
00:05:04.235 --> 00:05:09.120
A module of its own, building on the
appendix script in Access and Privilege,

77
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.271
with one reviewed file per
role, twenty-two in all,

78
00:05:12.271 --> 00:05:16.210
so a reviewer reads a change to
a file as a change to the tenant.

79
00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:21.089
Approvers appear by alias, and a small
bindings file per tenant turns each

80
00:05:21.089 --> 00:05:26.048
alias into a real group, so the same
files serve a test tenant and production.

81
00:05:26.048 --> 00:05:28.211
Every file sits inside bounds.

82
00:05:28.211 --> 00:05:33.147
Where Learn's pages disagree on the longest
activation, the code takes the stricter

83
00:05:33.147 --> 00:05:38.150
eight hours, and the owner capped standing
active assignments at fifteen days.

84
00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.800
Before anything is
written, nine checks run.

85
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.133
The first two are the two halves of
the lockout: approval with no approver,

86
00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:52.888
and too few emergency accounts
holding Global Administrator.

87
00:05:52.888 --> 00:05:56.592
Others keep approvers separate
from the people they approve,

88
00:05:56.592 --> 00:06:01.387
keep every file inside its bounds, and
refuse a wave until the earlier ones match.

89
00:06:01.387 --> 00:06:03.238
Any finding blocks the write.

90
00:06:03.238 --> 00:06:05.126
One check can't be automated:

91
00:06:05.126 --> 00:06:09.643
whether the activation gate's policy
is on lives in a beta property,

92
00:06:09.643 --> 00:06:14.690
so a person has to state it, with a flag,
after Chapter 3's run shows it's on.

93
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.265
Reading and writing are different jobs
here, with different permissions.

94
00:06:22.265 --> 00:06:27.040
Every read uses a read-only permission,
and the client refuses anything but a

95
00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:31.815
read unless it was built for writing,
which only happens when you ask to apply.

96
00:06:31.815 --> 00:06:35.710
Rule updates and new eligibility
need two write permissions;

97
00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:39.453
removing a standing assignment
is a third task with its own.

98
00:06:39.453 --> 00:06:43.546
Learn's PowerShell guide and the
tutorial ask for broader permissions,

99
00:06:43.546 --> 00:06:46.877
so the code follows the narrower
reference pages instead.

100
00:06:46.877 --> 00:06:51.140
And a test makes sure the dry run's
app never holds a write permission.

101
00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:57.965
This is the write path,
and it's careful by design.

102
00:06:57.965 --> 00:07:02.675
First it reads each role's policy, then
compares the seven rules with the file,

103
00:07:02.675 --> 00:07:05.815
after smoothing out
differences that don't matter.

104
00:07:05.815 --> 00:07:08.840
The plan lists every
change and sends nothing.

105
00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:12.515
Only when you ask to apply
does it write: rules first,

106
00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:16.452
then eligibility, so each
request meets the new limits.

107
00:07:16.452 --> 00:07:18.449
Then it reads the tenant again,

108
00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:21.803
and that read-back is the
evidence, not the response.

109
00:07:21.803 --> 00:07:25.573
Converting standing access
follows the workbook's order:

110
00:07:25.573 --> 00:07:29.870
eligible first, activation
confirmed, and only then removed.

111
00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:35.033
Who is this chapter for?

112
00:07:35.033 --> 00:07:38.571
Developers build the write
path, and they need PowerShell,

113
00:07:38.571 --> 00:07:42.257
the file format, and a sense of
what an eligible assignment is.

114
00:07:42.257 --> 00:07:48.183
The identity engineers own PIM itself: the
role files, the bindings, and the groups.

115
00:07:48.183 --> 00:07:52.442
And the information system security
officers review the changes,

116
00:07:52.442 --> 00:07:57.708
because every file is read by two people
and every apply follows a reviewed dry run.

117
00:07:57.708 --> 00:08:01.386
The identities are split the
same way: a reader for dry runs,

118
00:08:01.386 --> 00:08:06.800
a writer for applying, and the removal
permission held only for a conversion run.

119
00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:16.537
Lab 1 needs only a laptop: PowerShell
7 and Pester, no tenant and no network.

120
00:08:16.537 --> 00:08:20.951
First you send Learn's own samples to
the fake and watch thirteen tests pass,

121
00:08:20.951 --> 00:08:24.439
including the one that shows
the samples applying cleanly

122
00:08:24.439 --> 00:08:27.500
and still leaving a role six
rules away from its file.

123
00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:32.650
Then you list each task's permissions,
and see that a dry run needs only reads.

124
00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:36.839
Finally you dry-run the pilot
wave: nine roles, no findings,

125
00:08:36.839 --> 00:08:43.250
a plan of rule updates, eligibility requests
and two conversions, and nothing sent.

126
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.288
Now read what came out.

127
00:08:48.288 --> 00:08:51.844
One role was partly set by
hand, so its diff is short and

128
00:08:51.844 --> 00:08:55.887
touches only what's wrong; what
already matches isn't touched.

129
00:08:55.887 --> 00:08:59.730
The findings name the people holding
standing access, an automation

130
00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.976
account with no documented owner, and
someone eligible outside their group.

131
00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:07.389
Then you try Tier zero too
early, and the run blocks,

132
00:09:07.389 --> 00:09:10.387
one finding for each
pilot role not yet done.

133
00:09:10.387 --> 00:09:14.816
Applying the pilot wave stops once to
make you confirm the activation gate,

134
00:09:14.816 --> 00:09:18.101
then sends everything and
reads back zero differences.

135
00:09:18.101 --> 00:09:19.960
The whole suite passes.

136
00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:28.122
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant, never
production, and works one role.

137
00:09:28.122 --> 00:09:31.803
First the prerequisites:
the emergency accounts pass,

138
00:09:31.803 --> 00:09:35.811
and the activation gate's policy
is truly on, not report-only.

139
00:09:35.811 --> 00:09:38.998
You create the approver
group and the role's group,

140
00:09:38.998 --> 00:09:41.097
and two apps, a reader and a writer.

141
00:09:41.097 --> 00:09:44.748
Dry-run as the reader and review
the plan with a second person,

142
00:09:44.748 --> 00:09:47.960
then apply as the writer
and expect zero differences.

143
00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:53.260
Next, convert one standing assignment the
safe way, after a confirmed activation.

144
00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:57.750
And finally, restore the rules
from the saved copy and clean up.

145
00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.700
Each of these dangers has a
guard that runs every time.

146
00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:06.783
The two worst are the lockout halves,

147
00:10:06.783 --> 00:10:10.325
and both are checked against the
live tenant before any write.

148
00:10:10.325 --> 00:10:15.385
A context switched on before its policy is
on looks protective but enforces nothing,

149
00:10:15.385 --> 00:10:17.987
which is why a person
has to confirm the gate.

150
00:10:17.987 --> 00:10:22.765
Then there are the quieter ones: a script
nobody reviewed, one identity that can

151
00:10:22.765 --> 00:10:27.763
change everything, settings drifting in the
portal, and a back-out done from memory.

152
00:10:27.763 --> 00:10:32.730
The pattern holds: rules and saved
files, not people remembering.

153
00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:37.830
Here's what done looks like.

154
00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:42.217
Every role file has had two reviewers,
the bindings name every group,

155
00:10:42.217 --> 00:10:44.880
and no approver group can approve itself.

156
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.853
The emergency accounts pass,

157
00:10:46.853 --> 00:10:50.880
and the activation gate's policy
is on before anyone says so.

158
00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.292
The reader, the writer and the conversion
run each hold only what they need.

159
00:10:55.292 --> 00:10:59.256
Every apply followed a reviewed
dry run and read back clean,

160
00:10:59.256 --> 00:11:04.756
every removal has a confirmed activation,
and every run folder is kept as evidence.

161
00:11:04.756 --> 00:11:09.930
Next, Chapter 5 builds PIM for Groups,
the groups that hold this eligibility.

162
00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.090
Thanks for watching.
